        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ba'athism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1940 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, generally counted its founder or chief contributor; Salah al-Din al-Bitar, who worked directly with Aflaq; Zaki al-Arsuzi, who influenced Aflaq and founded the Arab Ba'ath; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a>, whose policies gave rise to Assadism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>, whose politics gave rise to Saddamism</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a>'s essay compilation Fi Sabil al-Ba'ath (The Road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, 1940), the party's main ideological book for more than two decades; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a> Charter of 1947, whose Articles 26–37 set out its socialism</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">Arab nationalism and the unity of the Arab nation; baʽth, the renaissance of Arab culture, values and society; a vanguard party ruling for an unspecified time; Arab socialism with state ownership of natural resources and the principal means of production; revolution against the reactionary classes; a secular state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> kept spiritual; the slogan &quot;Unity, Freedom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>&quot;</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Both Ba'athist regimes fell: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s in 2003 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s on 8 December 2024; the Ba'ath party in Syria was dissolved in 2025, few Ba'athist groups remain elsewhere with limited influence, and organisations linked to the party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> face forced dissolution by the government</span></div>
