    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ba'athism joined Arab nationalism to a socialism that rejected Marxist class struggle, and held that a vanguard Ba'ath party should rule for an unspecified time to build an &quot;enlightened&quot; Arab society.</li><li>The founders' party split in 1966 into a Syrian-dominated and an Iraqi-dominated movement, and the Assad family and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> built personalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> in the two countries.</li><li>Both Ba'athist regimes built police states with mass surveillance and ideological indoctrination, and both fell: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s in the invasion of 2003, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s on 8 December 2024.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, a Christian, called the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the seventh century the first Arab renaissance; when he died in 1989 the Iraqi Regional Command announced that he had converted to Islam, but his family was said not to know of any conversion.</p></aside>
    </section>

