    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">Zaki al-Arsuzi founds the Arab Ba'ath on 29 November, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a> and Salah al-Din al-Bitar found the Arab Ihya Movement, later the Arab Ba'ath Movement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">The first party congress meets, the two Ba'ath movements merge, and the party adopts its charter</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">The Military Committee of the Syrian Ba'ath party launches a coup that sets up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text">A second Syrian coup overthrows the old guard led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a> and Bitar and splits the movement into a Syrian and an Iraqi wing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1968</span><span class="rf-text">The Iraqi Ba'ath seizes power in a coup and later gives asylum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970</span><span class="rf-text">The Corrective Revolution puts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a> in power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">The invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> topples <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>, and the Iraqi Ba'ath party is banned</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2024</span><span class="rf-text">The Assad regime falls on 8 December, ending Ba'athist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq.jpg" alt="Michel Aflaq" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Aflaq_1963.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Basch, Fritz, CC BY-SA 3.0 nl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ba'athism originated in the political thought of the Syrian philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, Salah al-Din al-Bitar and Zaki Arsuzi, who are counted as its founders although they formed different organisations. In 1939 they and three others briefly tried to establish a party, which likely failed through personal animosity between Arsuzi and Aflaq. Arsuzi, an Arab from Alexandretta, was inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, the German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> movements and the Japanese economic "miracle", and was influenced by Hegel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>, Nietzsche and Oswald Spengler. He founded the Arab National Party in 1939, which dissolved that year, and on 29 November 1940 the Arab Ba'ath.</p><p>Aflaq and the more junior Bitar founded the Arab Ihya Movement in 1940, renamed the Arab Ba'ath Movement in 1943. Arsuzi suspected it of being an imperialist plot to undercut his influence by creating a movement of the same name. The two movements sparred over the 1941 coup of Rashid Ali Al-Gaylani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>: Aflaq's backed the government and sent volunteers against the British, while Arsuzi opposed the coup. His party lost members to Aflaq's, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> French authorities expelled him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1941. Aflaq's movement held its first party congress in 1947 and merged with Arsuzi's Arab Ba'ath; socialist values, present from the start, were stressed after the merger with Akram Al-Hawrani's Arab Socialist Movement in 1953.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ba'athism advocates a unified Arab state under a Ba'athist vanguard party and a revolutionary socialist framework, in the slogan "Unity, Freedom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a>, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">Sati</a>' al-Husri, held that language was the principal unifying factor of the Arab nation, and history another. At the centre of his thought was baʽth, "renaissance": the first Arab renaissance had been the emergence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the seventh century, and a new one would come by uniting the Arab nation. He blamed Arab states' decline on "feudalism, sectarianism, regionalism, intellectual reactionism", and held that only revolutionaries pure and devoted could cure them. Because reactionary classes ruled most Arab states, he later proposed that revolution spread from one country to the next, the transformed countries then uniting.</p><p>Aflaq supported a Leninist vanguard party built on democratic centralism, which could initiate policy even against a majority, since the population was still shaped by the old moral system. His liberty was authoritarian: a party that was not elected would ensure it, since the party had the common good at heart. The 1947 Charter placed natural resources, the principal means of production and transport in the hands of the state and abolished foreign concessions. Aflaq admired Marxist tenets but rejected dialectical materialism as the only truth, and held that in the Arab world all classes struggled against foreign capitalist domination. A Christian, he wished Islam to be "not imposed" on state and society, opposed atheism and fundamentalism, and supported secular government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad.jpg" alt="Hafez al-Assad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hafez_al-Assad_official_portrait_(precise_crop)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Government of Syria, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ba'athism took the form of two rival parties, one Syrian-led and one Iraqi-led. After the 1963 coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the party that took power was dominated by radical military officers known as the "Neo-Ba'ath", who put the establishment of a socialist state before pan-Arab unity. The coup of 1966 led by Salah Jadid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a> overthrew the old guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a> and Bitar, and many Syrian Ba'athist leaders defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Scholar Ofra Bengio claims that the pro-Syrian movement then took Arsuzi as the father of Ba'athist thought, while the pro-Iraqi movement kept Aflaq. Bitar left the movement in 1968, and Aflaq, who remained, became the ideologue of the Iraqi movement but was sidelined politically.</p><p>The Syrian government under Jadid stopped working for unity, took up terms such as "class struggle" and "people's war", and drew close to the Syrian Communist Party and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Hafez al-Assad took power in 1970, and Assadism took shape in Syria; in Iraq Saddamism took shape around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>. Hostilities between the two movements lasted until Hafez al-Assad's death in 2000, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a> pursued reconciliation with Iraq. The Syrian party also held influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> during Syria's occupation of that country. The National Vanguard Party, tied to the Iraqi Ba'ath party, operated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein.jpg" alt="Saddam Hussein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saddam_Hussein_in_1998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Iraqi News Agency, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ba'athism ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1963 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> after the 1968 coup. The Assad family and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> came to dominate the two parties and built personalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a>. The two regimes built police states that enforced mass surveillance and ideological indoctrination, subordinated student organisations, trade unions and other civil society to the party and the state, pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">Arabisation</a> of ethnic minorities, and legitimised their rule with conspiratorial anti-Zionist and anti-Western sentiment. The 1966 coup in Syria cost approximately 400 lives; the Jadid regime banned religious preaching and persecuted the clergy, whom neo-Ba'athists saw as class enemies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a>, after 1970, purged the armed forces, secret police, security forces and bureaucracy and installed loyalists, building a "coup-proof" system on nepotism and ethnic favouritism, with an Alawitisation of the party and the military. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a>'s crackdown on the 2011 Syrian revolution was brutal and led to a deadly civil war. The Simon Wiesenthal Center reported that the Nazi war criminal Alois Brunner died in Syria in 2010 under Bashar al-Assad's asylum, having advised both Assads on torture and on purging Syria's Jewish community; the regime rejected such accusations.</p><p>In Iraq party documents ordered branches to check the Arabic origin of prospective wives and their families. During the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> the party moved against members of non-Arab, especially Iranian, origin, and Saddam Hussein agreed with a Secretariat recommendation that people of Iranian origin not be admitted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historian Paul Salem called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a>'s authoritarian view of liberty, with an unelected party as its guarantor, weak in an "obvious" way, and some, Salem among them, saw his Arab nationalism as romantic and poetic. Aflaq's own followers judged the regimes harshly. Munif al-Razzaz, a former secretary general of the National Command, held that from 1961 there were two parties, a military one that was Ba'athist only in name, and Bitar said the 1966 coup "marked the end of Ba'athist politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>". Aflaq said, "I no longer recognise my party!" Jamal al-Atassi, a co-founder of Arsuzi's party and later a dissident, called Assadism a false nationalism, the domination of a minority, and said its state was run by a class that had made a fortune without contributing.</p><p>The historian of fascism Cyprian Blamires holds that Ba'athism may have been a Middle Eastern variant of fascism, pointing to its mix of illiberal nationalism and non-Marxist socialism, its elitist revolutionary vision, its wish to create a "new man" and its centralised authoritarian party. Others reply that Aflaq was active in the Syrian–Lebanese Communist Party and the French Communist Party and was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>. Members of Arsuzi's party read Nazi literature, and Arsuzi himself believed in the racial superiority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>; he refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s advances and did not support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. The Ba'ath regimes have also been accused of promoting an aggressive Arab ultranationalism, and the Mauritanian government accused the National Vanguard Party of racism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Aflaq</a>'s views were largely synonymous with the party's ideology until the mid-1950s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the early 1960s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and his Fi Sabil al-Ba'ath was its primary ideological book for over two decades. He is today considered the founder of the movement or at least its most notable contributor. Ba'athism sympathised with the non-aligned politics of Nehru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and opposed affiliation with either the Western or the Eastern bloc in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. In practice, Syria's state ideology became neo-Ba'athism and Iraq's was dominated by Saddamism, which fused ancient Babylonian and Assyrian heritage with Arab nationalism.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> was toppled in 2003 and the Iraqi Ba'ath party was banned under the policy of De-Ba'athification. In Syria the fall of the Assad regime on 8 December 2024 ended Ba'athist rule, and the party there was dissolved in 2025. The fall of the two regimes also marked the end of its ideological influence. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, where the Syrian party once held significant power and now derives influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a>, organisations linked to the party face forced dissolution by the government, and very few Ba'athist groups remain in other countries, with limited influence.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ba'athism is an Arab nationalist and socialist ideology, drawn up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 1940s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a>, Salah al-Din al-Bitar and Zaki al-Arsuzi, which called for a vanguard party to unite the Arab nation. Its two states, Syria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, became police states under the Assad family and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>, and both fell, in 2003 and 2024.</em></p>

